WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.630
Hi, I'm Pawl!

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:06.003
Flint is Recursive's first model, a mixture of experts for coding

00:00:06.003 --> 00:00:07.230
on your own machine.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.070
Here is why it is fast.

00:00:10.308 --> 00:00:13.591
The idea: every new token streams its weights through memory.

00:00:13.591 --> 00:00:17.202
So speed is bandwidth divided by bytes, and Flint is built

00:00:17.202 --> 00:00:18.515
to read fewer bytes.

00:00:20.508 --> 00:00:24.418
A token enters attention, which reads the cache earlier tokens left.

00:00:24.838 --> 00:00:28.974
A router scores two hundred fifty six small experts and wakes only six.

00:00:28.974 --> 00:00:30.564
The rest are never loaded.

00:00:30.984 --> 00:00:33.675
The output head turns that into the next token.

00:00:33.675 --> 00:00:36.067
Recursive stores even the head in four bits.

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:40.893
Each token touches two and a half billion parameters, out of almost forty billion.

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.573
Thirty five layers only look back over a short window, so

00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:46.751
their cache stops growing.

00:00:47.171 --> 00:00:51.302
Fourteen layers see the whole context, so their cache grows with every token.

00:00:52.858 --> 00:00:56.658
Divide them and the ceiling is about one hundred eighty tokens a second.

00:00:57.078 --> 00:01:00.684
An M4 Pro moves two hundred seventy three gigabytes a second.

00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:04.357
Flint's four bit weights cost about one and a half gigabytes per

00:01:04.357 --> 00:01:05.441
token, plus the cache.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:10.456
Recursive reports about one hundred thirty one with its own engine.

00:01:10.456 --> 00:01:14.081
On another engine the same model is much slower, so part of

00:01:14.081 --> 00:01:15.592
the speed is the kernels.

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:21.128
Fit Recursive's whole curve to that formula and the engine holds about

00:01:21.128 --> 00:01:24.125
two hundred gigabytes a second at every context length.

00:01:25.308 --> 00:01:28.525
Once the active body is small, the output head and the cache

00:01:28.525 --> 00:01:30.133
are what is left to shrink.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:30.937
Flint shrinks both.

00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:37.854
Six of two hundred fifty six experts, a four bit head, short windows.

00:01:37.854 --> 00:01:39.535
Speed is bandwidth over bytes.

00:01:39.955 --> 00:01:41.926
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.926 --> 00:01:42.489
I'm Pawl.

00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:42.770
Bye!
